{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-84108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-84108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=3t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/cdd531ad1df161f1c345c053c51e70fb522178f83688d1bdd07096bbdd40df00.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274746" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-7,-7)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7=2pt \\\\ -7=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{7}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =p{(-\\frac{7}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -7 & =p\\cdot \\frac{49}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{49}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{7}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{7}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1