\\\\ 4p & =-8 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-2{t}^{2},\\,y=-4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-280223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280223" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280223" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{8}{5}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/8e85666a72dde1ebbe24ff3d8dbb1404f010e81d9406aec790be11416b705f00.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-84110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "m