ligned}4px & =28x \\\\ 4p & =28 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(7){t}^{2},\\,y=2(7)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2},\\,y=14t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-280227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7t^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280227" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280227" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=3t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t"] ["math" "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"] ["math" "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2x,\\,{t}^{2}=-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =-4y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=-y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-106438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=-y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=5{t}^{2},\\,y=10t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{5},\\,t=\\frac{y}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =20x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=20x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-274758" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-274758" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-10{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x}{10},\\,t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x}{10}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{x}{10} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-280365" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-280365" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{2}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=8y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}=2y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=6y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=8y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{8}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=8y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-4x,\\,{t}^{2}=-8y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =16{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-8y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2} & =-8y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06a4652519955da98e3c4fbcc8f81c3d81643ef2348865d89f522fda7d11ab4c"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{8}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{8}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "q-84107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2c33a1aad6d2b018a34402c1c0ce2a1aca3cb982eb5bcdd75f89c0be4e04705"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "55ec1fc4f19347fcb9671666c8adb2d34d9f4efed717ac3a6445363831fc3bd9"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "55ec1fc4f19347fcb9671666c8adb2d34d9f4efed717ac3a6445363831fc3bd9"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f"}] "usage" {"e-7436" [] "e-7442" [] "q-84107" [] "q-84110" [] "e-7437" [] "q-84108" []} "basis" 1078 "retractions" [[17592186088374 :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)$?"] [17592186088376 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/875/Source/Images/q-84110.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the parabola shown above?"] [17592186088373 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x =-\\frac{1}{4}t, y =-\\frac{1}{8}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."] [17592186087567 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/875/Source/Images/e77c2a7f7fa0a1f9a45e1aa139b3a543.png)"] [17592186087567 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0)$, and passes through the point $(4,8)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = 2pt; 8 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= 2pt \\\\\n2 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{2}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= p(\\frac{2}{p})^{2} \\\\\n8 &= p \\cdot \\frac{4}{p^{2}} \\\\\n8 &= \\frac{4}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2\n$$"] [17592186087568 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x$?"] [17592186087568 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4px &= -64x \\\\\n4p &= -64 \\\\\np &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t\n$$"] [17592186087569 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$."] [17592186087569 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{x}{2}, t = \\frac{y}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{y^{2}}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny^{2} &= 8x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186087169 17592186087170 17592186087171 17592186087172 17592186087173 17592186087175 17592186087176 17592186087177 17592186087178 17592186087179 17592186087187 17592186087188 17592186087189 17592186087190 17592186087191] "immutable_fields" [[17592186087168 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087168 :answer-field/id] [17592186087168 :answer-field/key] [17592186087168 :answer-field/type] [17592186087174 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087174 :answer-field/id] [17592186087174 :answer-field/key] [17592186087174 :answer-field/type] [17592186087186 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087186 :answer-field/id] [17592186087186 :answer-field/key] [17592186087186 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
